.TH SORTOF-EXTENSION 1 "October 2026" "sortof-suite" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sortof\-extension \- list files in natural order by extension
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sortof\-extension
[options] SOURCE...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Writes a list of files, one path per line, sorted by extension (case ignored, files without an extension first), then by file name, then by folder, so that same\-named files in different folders sit together. This suits a collection of games or documents: all the .exe files together, then all the .dat files, and a runtime file that many games carry ends up in one place. Nothing is hashed or read.
.PP
A source is a directory (scanned recursively), a regular file (listed as it is), or \- for a list of paths on standard input, used once. A path that comes up more than once is listed once.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-p PATH
Pin PATH: it comes first in the output, exactly as given, and is never sorted, hashed or read. May be repeated. A file named cdcstore.idx is pinned by itself.
.TP
.B \-f PATH
Write the list to PATH instead of standard output.
.TP
.B \-q
Quiet.
.TP
.B \-h
Print help.
